{
  "title": "Coordinators C04: Claim client",
  "body": "Part of #902. Slice C04 is the claim client. It writes claim refs in the public repository with GitHub's compare-and-swap, classifies every outcome, and defines the claim record. It wires nothing into runs:\n- #952 and #968 build leases on it;\n- #960 and #963 use it to checkpoint and adopt.\n\n## Probe status\nDone. #902 records the owner-token and App-token probes (2026-10-27):\n- GraphQL `updateRefs` with `beforeOid` is a compare-and-swap;\n- REST `PATCH git/refs` with `force: false` is not one on `refs/limitless/*`;\n- REST create returns 422 on an existing ref;\n- empty trees are refused;\n- commits without dates get GitHub's time;\n- no Actions run starts;\n- a lost compare-and-swap reports only \"Something went wrong\";\n- `gh api --include` on a missing ref (checked 2026-10-28, gh 2.101.0) exits 1, prints `HTTP/2.0 404 Not Found` as the first stdout line, and writes `gh: Not Found (HTTP 404)` to stderr. The client reads the status line from `CommandError.stdout`, and the fake reproduces exactly this shape.\n\n## Invariants\n1. **Compare-and-swap writes only.** Every claim write is one `updateRefs` with `beforeOid` (40 zeros for a create) and `force: false`. REST PATCH, DELETE and `force: true` are never sent.\n2. **Four outcomes, decided in a fixed order.** After a write error, the client reads once and classifies, in this order:\n   1. read failed → `unconfirmed`;\n   2. tip still `beforeOid` (or absent for a create) → `unchanged`;\n   3. tip is our new commit → `applied`, anchored;\n   4. another commit whose record equals **every** requested field → `applied`, with `anchor: null`;\n   5. anything else → `moved`.\n\n   A successful response is `applied`, anchored. Because `unchanged` is decided first, a rejected renewal never confirms itself, even when the old tip names the same holder.\n2a. **One total classification boundary.** Every step of `read` and `write` is inside one boundary that maps every failure, so no exception leaves the client except cancellation:\n   - the tree POST;\n   - the repository-id query;\n   - the commit POST;\n   - `updateRefs`;\n   - the ref read;\n   - the commit read;\n   - every JSON or record parse.\n\n   | Failure | Result |\n   |---|---|\n   | The caller's `signal` aborted | the `AbortError` is rethrown unchanged; that isn't an outcome |\n   | Any pre-compare-and-swap step fails (transport error, non-2xx, malformed JSON, a missing field) | `unchanged`, with no `updateRefs` sent |\n   | `updateRefs` fails or returns a malformed response | confirm by reading, then classify by invariant 2 |\n   | A read step fails or returns malformed data | `unconfirmed` (for `write`) or `{ kind: \"unconfirmed\" }` (for `read`) |\n   | A ref read with HTTP status 404 | `absent` |\n\n   **How a 404 is detected.** Reads call `gh api --include`. The status line is parsed from stdout, whether the command exits 0 or not (`CommandError.stdout`). Only a parsed `HTTP/… 404` status line means `absent`, never an error string. Any other status, or no status line, is `unconfirmed`.\n3. **Ownership is read from records, never assumed.** A tuple is (kind, generation, holder, run). A malformed record matches nothing.\n4. **No local clock in any GitHub decision.** Commit dates come from GitHub. The client stores a local sample only as an opaque anchor for #951.\n5. **Public records carry only opaque coordination data:**\n   - kind;\n   - the issue or PR number (already in the ref name);\n   - state;\n   - generation;\n   - member id;\n   - run id;\n   - run status;\n   - the opaque checkpoint pointer.\n\n   There is no branch name: a run branch's slug comes from the private request, and branch and PR identity live in the private manifest. There is also no request, report, policy digest, endpoint or hostname. The commit author is a fixed synthetic identity.\n6. **Instance-only state.** Caches live on the client instance, so two coordinators can share one test process.\n7. **Inert.** Nothing constructs a client outside tests until a later slice wires it.\n\n## Mechanism (new `src/integrations/claim-ref.ts`)\n### Interface\n```ts\nexport type ClaimKind = \"issue\" | \"pr\" | \"run\";            // \"land\" arrives with C17 (deferred); until then it is malformed\nexport type ClaimRecord = {\n  kind: ClaimKind; state: \"held\" | \"released\"; generation: number;\n  number?: number;           // required for issue and pr kinds: binds the record to its ref\n  holder: string;            // member id, ^m-[0-9a-f]{16}$\n  run: string;               // logical run id, stable across holders\n  status: RunStatus;\n  checkpoint?: string;       // ^ckpt1:[0-9a-f]{32}:[0-9a-f]{64}$ — opaque here; produced by #960\n};                           // no branch or PR fields: those are private (#955, #960)\nexport type ClaimTip = { sha: string; committedAt: string; record: ClaimRecord | \"malformed\" };\nexport type ClaimRead = { kind: \"absent\" } | { kind: \"tip\"; tip: ClaimTip } | { kind: \"unconfirmed\"; error: string };\nexport type Anchor = { inclusive: number; exclusive: number };   // clock samples; meaning in #951\nexport type WriteOutcome =\n  | { kind: \"applied\"; tip: ClaimTip; anchor: Anchor | null }\n  | { kind: \"unchanged\"; tip: ClaimTip | null }\n  | { kind: \"moved\"; tip: ClaimTip }\n  | { kind: \"unconfirmed\"; error: string };\nexport interface ClaimClient {\n  read(repo: string, ref: string, signal?: AbortSignal): Promise\u003cClaimRead\u003e;\n  write(repo: string, ref: string, before: ClaimTip | null, record: ClaimRecord,\n        opts?: { signal?: AbortSignal; beforeSend?: () =\u003e boolean; onCommit?: (sha: string) =\u003e void }): Promise\u003cWriteOutcome\u003e;\n  // beforeSend: called synchronously just before the updateRefs request; false → nothing sent, outcome \"unchanged\".\n  // onCommit: called with the new commit SHA before updateRefs (#952 journals it).\n}\nexport function createClaimClient(deps: { gh: GhRunner; sample: () =\u003e Anchor }): ClaimClient;\nexport function formatRecord(r: ClaimRecord): string;\nexport function parseRecord(message: string, ref: string): ClaimRecord | \"malformed\";\nexport function memberId(store: Store): string;   // settings \"member.id\", created once\n```\n**`parseRecord` checks the ref too.**\n- A `kind: issue` record with `number: n` must sit at `refs/limitless/claims/\u003cn\u003e`, `pr` with `number: n` at `claims/pr-\u003cn\u003e`, and `run` at `claims/run-\u003crun\u003e` with a matching `run`.\n- A mismatch is `malformed`.\n\n### Operations (all through `deps.gh` with the repository argument, so the App identity applies)\n- **Tree:** `POST repos/\u003crepo\u003e/git/trees` with the one file `claim` (`limitless claim\\n`), cached per repository on the instance.\n- **Commit:** `POST git/commits` with that tree, `parents: [before.sha]` (none for a create), and author and committer `limitless \u003cfactory@example.invalid\u003e`, with no dates.\n  - `deps.sample()` is taken immediately before sending.\n  - A commit-POST failure is `unchanged`, because no ref write was sent.\n- **Ref write:** `api graphql --input -`:\n  ```\n  mutation($r:ID!,$u:[RefUpdate!]!){updateRefs(input:{repositoryId:$r,refUpdates:$u}){clientMutationId}}\n  ```\n  with `{name: ref, beforeOid, afterOid, force: false}`. The repository node id comes from `repository(owner:, name:) { id }`, cached on the instance.\n- **Read:**\n  1. `GET git/ref/\u003cref without \"refs/\"\u003e` with `--include`; 404 is detected as in invariant 2a.\n  2. Then `GET git/commits/\u003csha\u003e` for the raw message and `committer.date`.\n  - The **raw commit data** is cached by SHA on the instance; it is immutable.\n  - **Record validation isn't cached:** `parseRecord(message, ref)` runs on every read, against the requested ref, because one commit can be the tip of several refs.\n  - The namespace is never listed, so any number of historical refs is fine.\n- **Message:** `limitless-claim: 1`, then one `key: value` line per present field, parsed by a strict zod schema. Unknown keys, missing required keys and bad values are `malformed`.\n- **Member id:** `settings` key `member.id`, set to `m-` plus 16 random hex digits on first use. It is never derived from a hostname, because claim commits are public.\n\n## Tests\n- **The fake:** a `GhRunner` holding refs in memory, with the following, plus a failure injector at each step:\n  - `git/trees`, which refuses an empty tree;\n  - `git/commits`, with dates from a fake GitHub clock;\n  - `git/ref` and `git/commits/\u003csha\u003e`;\n  - `updateRefs` with exact `beforeOid` compare-and-swap, which rejects non-fast-forward updates and answers every failure with \"Something went wrong\".\n- Any REST PATCH or DELETE of a ref, or `force: true`, fails the test. No network.\n- **Protocol checks are made against the request, not the fake's enforcement.** Each test asserts the emitted `beforeOid`: the tip just read, or 40 zeros for a create. Tests that need a mutant use an **unconditional-ref-write** fake that applies any write regardless of `beforeOid` and fast-forwarding. A separate case covers a stale `beforeOid` against the strict fake.\n\n1. **Concurrent create:** one client gets `applied`; the other gets `moved`, showing the winner's tip.\n2. **Outcomes, for create, renew and release:**\n   - (a) A success gives `applied` with an anchor.\n   - (b) An error, then our commit at the tip, gives `applied`.\n   - (c) An error, then another commit with every requested field, gives `applied` with `anchor: null`.\n   - (d) An error with the tip unchanged gives `unchanged`.\n   - (e) An error with a different record gives `moved`.\n   - (f) An error and a failed read give `unconfirmed`.\n   - (g) A failed commit POST gives `unchanged`, and no `updateRefs` call is made.\n3. **A rejected renewal never confirms itself:** a renewal to `status: needs_human` fails, and the tip is still `beforeOid` with `status: running` and the same holder, run and generation. That is `unchanged`.\n4. **Same holder, different fields:** a tip with our holder, run and generation but another `status`, `state` or `checkpoint` is `moved`.\n5. **Delayed application:** the fake applies a write after reporting an error and after the client's read (`unchanged`). The next `read` shows our record.\n6. **Parsing:**\n   - Every field round-trips.\n   - These are each `malformed`: an unknown key (including `branch` or `pr`), kind `land`, a kind that doesn't match its ref, a bad member id, a bad checkpoint pointer, a non-integer generation, and free text.\n7. **Many refs:** with 101 historical refs under `refs/limitless/claims/`, reading any one ref works, and no listing call is made.\n8. **The tree:** an empty tree is never requested, and the tree is created once per repository per instance.\n9. **Two instances share nothing:** caches are independent, and a fresh instance re-reads.\n10. **Member id:** created once, stable across store reopen, and matching the pattern.\n11. **Compare-and-swap only:** each write is one `updateRefs` with a `beforeOid` and `force: false`, and a create sends 40 zeros.\n12. **No local clock:** with the fake GitHub clock ±1 h from any local clock, every outcome is identical. `sample()` values only appear as anchors.\n13. **Failures at each step before the compare-and-swap:** failing the tree POST, the repository-id query or the commit POST (transport, non-2xx, malformed JSON) gives `unchanged`. No `updateRefs` is sent.\n14. **Read failures:**\n    - A ref read with a 404 status line gives `absent`.\n    - A 500, a transport error, a malformed body and an error string that mentions \"404\" without a 404 status line each give `unconfirmed`.\n    - A failed commit read after a good ref read gives `unconfirmed`.\n15. **Cancellation:** an aborted signal at any step rethrows `AbortError` and returns no outcome.\n16. **A stale `beforeOid`:** writing with a `beforeOid` older than the current tip, against the strict fake, gives `moved` (the tip is the newer record), and the tip is unchanged. Against the unconditional mutant, the same write would apply; this case documents the protocol, and #968's race tests use the mutant.\n17. **The emitted expected OID:** for create, renew and release, the `updateRefs` request carries exactly the tip read beforehand (or zeros), checked against the request body.\n18. **Same SHA, wrong ref:** a commit whose record is `kind: issue, number: 12`, set as the tip of both `claims/12` and `claims/13`, reads valid at `claims/12` and `malformed` at `claims/13`. Only one commit fetch is made, from the cache.\n19. **Hooks:**\n    - `onCommit` receives the commit SHA before `updateRefs` is sent.\n    - `beforeSend` returning `false` sends no `updateRefs`, and the outcome is `unchanged`.\n\n## Compatibility\n- No migration: the member id is a `settings` key. No config and no behaviour change.\n- An older release is unaffected.\n\n## Out of scope\n- Leases, expiry and windows (#951, #952, #968).\n- Checkpoint pointers' meaning (#960).\n- Wiring into runs.\n- The land kind (C17 (deferred), deferred).\n\n## Budget\nAbout 180 source lines plus tests: types and record parse and format about 45; tree, commit, `updateRefs` and read about 60; the total classification boundary, status-line parsing, cancellation and hooks about 60; member id about 15.\n\nStop and report past 245.\n"
}
